## Microscopic work must use the boundary interaction

There is a more useful work check than multiplying trace error by the
entire growing reservoir norm. Write V_CS for all added terms touching the
old system; put all other source terms, including their reserves, in H_C.
Then the static model gives the exact identity, for every actual input,

    Delta <H_C> = -Delta <K> -Delta <V_CS>.

This is an operator-energy balance in the stated model. Its identification
with a physical energy observable is still a supplied identification, not
an axiom derived here. If every old vertex has added incident sum<=J_*,
then ||V_CS||<=2J_* for this two-qubit plant, independently of source size.
Term splitting cannot improve this bound or evade the incident accounting.

Let delta_i and delta_f be the actual old-state trace errors against the
specified ideal initial/final old states. Suppose actual initial and final
interaction expectations, relative to the same declared scalar v0, have
bounds beta_i and beta_f: |<V_CS>-v0|<=beta at each endpoint. A constant
reserve cancels in this energy *difference*, but remains fully installed,
counted in the cap, source moments and coherent phase. At a zero-control
endpoint of the external model, its ideal work is W_* = Delta<K>_ideal.
Because width(K)/2=1 in E0 units,

    |Delta<H_C>+W_*|
       <= E0*(delta_i+delta_f)+beta_i+beta_f.                 (1)

Both initial and final errors are required. For the present W, W_*=0.
If a comparison state certifies a boundary expectation beta^Q relative to
v0, and its full trace discrepancy is d, the actual bound is
beta^Q+||V_CS||d, since the equal-trace states cancel v0. A stronger
state-action estimate ||(V_CS-v0 I)Q(t)|| also bounds the corresponding
ideal expectation. Thus a finite post-runtime quiet
window needs an actual small-coupling-on-the-delivered-state certificate,
not a claim that the programmed external waveform is zero. Residual
controls and interaction energy are not removed by naming a baseline.

Equation(1) prices net source mean-energy transfer through bounded boundary
observables. It does *not* estimate source variance, all energy moments,
source phase coherence or an energy distribution from trace distance. Those
need explicit initial moments and appropriate output observables or weighted
error estimates. A's full-generator mean and variance are conserved, while
the source subsystem's moments need not be. In particular, a product/free-
clock comparison can be close in trace yet hide finite energy transfer in
a growing-energy tail. The actual interaction and conservation identity,
not an unproved moment comparison to chi(t), must carry this ledger.

