## Decisive first test and alternatives

Before attempting a large clock, test any proposed history-clock attachment
against the literal old drift. If G=sum_j |j><j| tensor V_j gauges a program
history hopping Hamiltonian into H_clock tensor I, then exactly

    G^dagger (I tensor K) G
       =sum_j |j><j| tensor (V_j^dagger K V_j).              (2)

Consequently adding I tensor K to a convenient history-clock construction
does not leave a free clock plus the same old K unless the relevant prefix
conjugates agree with K. Equation(2) is a direct block multiplication, not
a numerical diagnostic or a general impossibility theorem.

For the concrete old system, [K,I+X1]=2i Y1 Z2. As a transparent algebraic
probe, V_j=exp(-i theta X1) gives

    V_j^dagger K V_j
       =2I+cos(2theta) Z1 Z2+sin(2theta) Y1 Z2.

At theta=pi/4 the old interaction has become Y1 Z2 in that clock block.
The isolated X rotation is a probe of the clock identity, not an available
negative-drift operation. The actual first driven segment has
d(U(t)^dagger K U(t))/dt=i U(t)^dagger[K+a psi(t)P,K]U(t),
whose noncommuting control contribution is nonzero during its onset.
Thus the desired word's noncommuting prefixes cannot simply be ignored
because its final W commutes with K. Prefix drift terms can also be counted
twice if literal K is appended to gates that already include its evolution.

The proposed next decisive check is therefore **term-level and exact**:
derive the gauge transform for the proposed minimal local source, retain
the Y1 Z2 clock-conditioned term in (2), and verify on the complete space
how its effect is represented or canceled, with every physical term, reserve,
added incident norm and output included. Then derive the residual integral
and endpoint interaction bounds above. An encoded-sector-only equality,
an unpaid large penalty, or a three-body clock-controlled old edge would
fail this specific certificate. This test has not been executed as a new
fixture here; the displayed algebra is the analytical specification.

There are live alternatives. One may redesign the autonomous source in the
literal-drift interaction picture and explicitly price the resulting
clock-dependent interactions; use a direct local actuator with an
energy-carrying comparison state; or accept a different runtime and compile
its actual-time target with all new timing costs. These are proposed routes,
not routes attempted and ruled out. A supplied Feynman clock is not by
itself a solution to their locality, cap, energy and source-delivery
obligations. Similarly A remains a valid alternative if its growing
strengths are allowed. None of these observations establishes a universal
lower bound or an inability to realize a finite quantum controller.

Finite-window accuracy may include a moving source. It implies neither a
stationary output for all later times nor a reusable restored program.
Exact permanent halting, erasure and unlimited repetition are excluded from
this first specification. A successful finite-window source and equation(1)
would be a useful conditional milestone without those extra claims.

